    </ul>

    <p>Two-bedders are <strong>45%</strong> of the project (84 of 188) and the 4-bedders another <strong>30%</strong> (56 units across the two sizes). That is an unusually even split for a city project, and it tells you Aurea is pitched at two different buyers at once: the investor or professional couple taking a 2-bedder, and the established household taking a 4-bedder with a private lift. Worth knowing when you compare stacks: homes on levels 4&ndash;16 have 2.85&nbsp;m ceilings, while the &ldquo;H&rdquo; units on levels 18&ndash;32 have 3.02&nbsp;m.</p>

    <h2>The 5 selling angles you&rsquo;ll hear</h2>

    <p><strong>1. &ldquo;A conserved national icon.&rdquo;</strong> Aurea is part of the first collective sale and conservation of a modernist building in Singapore. You&rsquo;re buying into a landmark, not a generic tower.</p>

    <p><strong>2. &ldquo;Only 188 homes in a mixed-use development.&rdquo;</strong> Mixed-use projects usually run to 400&ndash;700+ units. Aurea is boutique by comparison.</p>

    <p><strong>3. &ldquo;Bay and city views, with three sky terraces.&rdquo;</strong> Facilities sit on levels 3, 17 and 33 &mdash; the Resort deck with the Grand Infinity pool, the Lounge deck with a private dining room and viewing point, and the Sky deck with the Sky Infinity pool and sky gym. Even city-facing 2-bedroom owners can take the bay view from the common areas.</p>

    <p><strong>4. &ldquo;Downtown Core with growth plans all around.&rdquo;</strong> Beach Road and the Ophir-Rochor corridor, the 8.2-hectare Kampong Bugis precinct, the Nicoll waterfront precinct, Kallang Alive and the North-South Corridor.</p>

    <p><strong>5. &ldquo;Blue-chip team and premium specs.&rdquo;</strong> Far East and Perennial as developers, DP Architects on design, private lifts from the 4-bedroom types upwards, Laufen and Franke fittings, and 3.02&nbsp;m ceilings on levels 18&ndash;32.</p>

    <p>All five are fair points. The question is how much each one should weigh for <em>your</em> household.</p>

    <h2>The honest review &mdash; NAVIS PrimeKey Analysis</h2>

    <p>NAVIS PrimeKey scores a home on eight pillars, each out of 5: MRT connectivity, growth hotspots, the GLS and en-bloc pipeline nearby, project size, remaining tenure, rental yield, school effect, and the MOP cluster (nearby HDB flats reaching their Minimum Occupation Period, a key source of upgrader buyers). It is a shortlisting tool. It shows where a project is strong and where you need to look harder &mdash; not what a unit is worth.</p>

